          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[08:20] Recall:</strong> How did Western military intervention in the Russian Civil War (1918–1920) establish long-term Soviet paranoia regarding capitalist encirclement?</li><li><strong>[21:40] Explain:</strong> Explain why Joseph Stalin was furious with Churchill and Roosevelt over the repeated delays in opening a \"Second Front\" in Western Europe between 1941 and 1944.</li><li><strong>[35:10] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate how the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945 transformed US-Soviet diplomacy from wartime partnership to nuclear brinkmanship.</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Britain, the US, France, and Japan sent expeditionary troops to Russia to support anti-communist \"White\" forces, convincing Lenin and Stalin that capitalist powers would always seek to militarily strangle the Soviet Union.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The USSR bore the overwhelming brunt of the Nazi war machine, suffering over 20 million deaths while fighting 80% of the German army alone on the Eastern Front. Stalin believed Britain and the US deliberately delayed D-Day (until June 1944) so that Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union would bleed each other to exhaustion.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Truman's atomic announcement at Potsdam and the destruction of Hiroshima and Nagasaki signaled to Moscow that the US possessed monopoly control over an apocalyptic superweapon. Rather than intimidating Stalin into geopolitical concessions in Poland, it accelerated Soviet paranoia, convincing Stalin that the USSR was in mortal danger and driving Soviet espionage to detonate its own atomic bomb by August 1949.</li></ol>"
