        "source_context": {
          "Source A": "Stalin’s Bolshoi speech shattered Western hopes that wartime cooperation would continue into peacetime. In Washington, Supreme Court Justice William O. Douglas described the address as 'The Declaration of World War Three,' prompting the US State Department to urgently demand diplomatic analysis. **Hinge Question:** Why did Western leaders interpret Stalin’s ideological speech as a direct military warning to the capitalist world?",
          "Source B": "Lenin’s core text established the bedrock doctrine taught in every Soviet school and university. To Western observers, this text proved that communist states were ideologically committed to the violent destruction of capitalist democracies. **Hinge Question:** If Soviet leaders were educated on Lenin's doctrine of violent revolution, why was it difficult for Western democracies to trust Soviet promises of peace?",
          "Source C": "David Low was widely regarded as the greatest political cartoonist of the 20th century. Here he visualizes Churchill's metaphor of the Iron Curtain, capturing the British public's growing dismay that wartime alliance with Russia had transformed into secretive hostility. **Hinge Question:** What visual symbols does the cartoonist use to show that the Soviet government was deliberately shutting itself off from the rest of the world?",
          "Source D": "Kennan’s telegram circulated through the highest levels of the Truman administration. Kennan argued that traditional diplomacy would fail with Moscow because Soviet leaders needed external enemies to justify their own tyrannical rule at home. **Hinge Question:** How did Kennan's claim that Soviet power is 'highly sensitive to logic of force' persuade President Truman to adopt a policy of military containment?",
          "Source E": "While Western observers saw Soviet elections as rubber-stamp shams, Soviet domestic propaganda relentlessly hammered home economic comparisons: emphasizing full employment and social welfare to contrast with memories of the 1930s Western Great Depression. **Hinge Question:** Why did Soviet propaganda focus so heavily on full employment and healthcare while completely ignoring the absence of political opposition?"
        },
