          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[09:15] Recall:</strong> What new Western currency reform in June 1948 directly prompted Stalin to sever all rail, road, and canal traffic into West Berlin?</li><li><strong>[18:40] Explain:</strong> Explain how Allied airmen like Lieutenant Gail Halvorsen ('The Chocolate Flyer') used small parachutes of candy to win the hearts and minds of Berlin children and transform Western public opinion.</li><li><strong>[31:20] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate how Stalin's Berlin Blockade backfired catastrophically against Soviet strategic interests, accelerating the creation of NATO and the Federal Republic of Germany (FRG).</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The introduction of the <em>Deutsche Mark</em> in the Western zones and West Berlin.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Halvorsen began dropping chocolate bars and bubblegum attached to tiny handkerchief parachutes to children gathered near Tempelhof runway. The initiative went viral across American media, prompting millions of donations of candy from US schoolchildren. It transformed a military-logistical operation into a powerful humanitarian symbol, destroying Soviet propaganda that framed the West as capitalist imperialists.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Stalin hoped to force the Western Allies to abandon West Berlin or abandon plans for an independent West German state. Instead, his aggressive tactics convinced Western leaders that the USSR was an immediate military threat. The crisis spurred the immediate unification of the three Western zones into the Federal Republic of Germany (May 1949) and led directly to the signing of the North Atlantic Treaty (NATO) in April 1949, committing US nuclear power to defend Western Europe under Article 5.</li></ol>"
