          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[08:15] Recall:</strong> What historic denunciation did Nikita Khrushchev deliver behind closed doors at the 20th Soviet Communist Party Congress in February 1956?</li><li><strong>[24:30] Explain:</strong> What three radical political demands were made by Imre Nagy and Hungarian protesters in October 1956 that provoked Soviet military intervention?</li><li><strong>[32:10] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate why President Eisenhower and the United Nations refused to provide military assistance to Hungarian freedom fighters despite Radio Free Europe broadcasts encouraging resistance.</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The \"Secret Speech\" (<em>On the Cult of Personality and Its Consequences</em>), which denounced Joseph Stalin's brutal purges, terror, and personality cult, inaugurating \"De-Stalinisation\".</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Nagy announced that Hungary was withdrawing from the Warsaw Pact to declare permanent neutrality, demanded the immediate withdrawal of all Soviet Red Army troops, and abolished the one-party state to hold free multi-party democratic elections.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> The US was constrained by the reality of Mutually Assured Destruction: sending US troops into Hungary would mean a direct land war with the Soviet Union inside its recognized sphere of influence, inevitably escalating to nuclear war. Furthermore, the crisis erupted simultaneously with the Suez Crisis (Britain, France, and Israel invading Egypt), which politically divided the Western allies and completely distracted world attention. Nagy was arrested and executed, demonstrating that the USSR would use ruthless military force to preserve its Eastern European satellite empire.</li></ol>"
