          "model_answer": "<p>The Cuban Missile Crisis of October 1962 was critically important for superpower relations because it brought the superpowers to the very precipice of thermonuclear destruction, compelling both leaders to recognise the mortal danger of brinkmanship. Having discovered Soviet ballistic missiles in Cuba, President Kennedy imposed a naval quarantine while Premier Khrushchev readied strategic forces. Confronted with the reality that an accidental clash could trigger global devastation, both leaders negotiated a peaceful compromise. Consequently, this near-catastrophe shattered their illusion that nuclear weapons could be deployed for diplomatic intimidation.</p><p>Furthermore, the crisis was important because it initiated a constructive era of institutionalized crisis management and nuclear arms limitation. Recognising that communication delays had nearly caused accidental war, the superpowers installed the direct Washington-Moscow 'Hotline' in June 1963 to facilitate rapid presidential dialogue during emergencies. In August 1963, they also signed the Partial Test Ban Treaty, prohibiting atmospheric and underwater nuclear testing. Therefore, the Cuban Missile Crisis was profoundly important because it catalysed the initial thaw in superpower hostility, directly laying the foundations for the détente of the 1970s.</p>"
