          "teacher_guidance": "Examines the escalating US commitment in Vietnam from the 1964 Gulf of Tonkin incident through Operation Rolling Thunder and search-and-destroy missions, to the 1968 Tet Offensive and US withdrawal. Recommended Watch Window: 00:30–04:45 • Lesson Phase: Guided Instruction & Archival Investigation. Tone Alert: Fast-paced military documentary with primary battlefield combat footage.",
          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:10] Recall:</strong> What 1964 congressional measure granted President Lyndon B. Johnson sweeping executive authority to deploy US combat troops to Vietnam without a formal declaration of war?</li><li><strong>[02:45] Explain:</strong> Explain how Vietcong guerrilla tactics—including subterranean tunnel systems, booby traps, and ambushes—neutralized superior American aerial firepower and chemical defoliants.</li><li><strong>[04:20] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate why the January 1968 Tet Offensive was simultaneously a crushing tactical defeat for communist forces, yet a decisive strategic and political victory that ended the US war effort.</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The Gulf of Tonkin Resolution.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The Vietcong avoided conventional pitched battles, adopting \"belt-grabbing\" tactics (fighting within yards of American soldiers to prevent artillery strikes). By operating out of extensive hidden underground tunnel complexes (such as at Cu Chi) and melting into civilian farming villages, they made US heavy bombers, napalm, and Agent Orange counterproductive, destroying civilian support for Saigon.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Militarily, communist forces suffered catastrophic losses (over 40,000 casualties) and failed to spark a civilian uprising, effectively wiping out the southern Vietcong. Politically, however, television footage of guerrillas storming the US Embassy in Saigon shattered President Johnson's claims that victory was imminent. Walter Cronkite's declaration that the war was in a stalemate destroyed public faith in the administration, forcing LBJ to halt bombing and announce he would not seek re-election.</li></ol>"
