          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[07:40] Recall:</strong> What famous phrase did Alexander Dubček use to describe his vision of reformed, democratic communism in Czechoslovakia during the 1968 Prague Spring?</li><li><strong>[22:15] Explain:</strong> Explain why Leonid Brezhnev and other Warsaw Pact leaders (like East Germany's Walter Ulbricht) feared that Czech freedom of speech and press would destroy the entire Soviet bloc.</li><li><strong>[35:40] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate the ideological significance of the <em>Brezhnev Doctrine</em> issued in November 1968: how did it redefine the sovereignty of Eastern European nations and justify military aggression within the socialist camp?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> \"Socialism with a Human Face\" (<em>Socialismus s lidskou tváří</em>).</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Dubček abolished state press censorship, allowing newspapers and television to expose Communist Party corruption, criticise the police, and debate democratic ideas. Brezhnev and Ulbricht feared that if Czechoslovakia opened its border to the West and allowed multi-party democracy, it would inevitably leave the Warsaw Pact, triggering a domino effect of anti-communist revolutions across Poland, Hungary, and East Germany.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> The Brezhnev Doctrine proclaimed that the sovereignty of individual socialist states was limited by their collective duty to protect the communist system. It declared that when forces hostile to socialism attempt to turn any socialist country toward capitalism, it becomes not only a problem for that country, but the common concern of all socialist countries, legally justifying Soviet military invasion to crush domestic reform. This permanently alienated Western communist parties and proved that Soviet control over Eastern Europe rested entirely on raw military coercion.</li></ol>"
