          "teacher_guidance": "Konrad H. Jarausch explores the construction of the Berlin Wall in August 1961 to stem the brain drain of 2.7 million East Germans, and its dramatic opening on 9 November 1989. Recommended Watch Window: 00:30–04:45 • Lesson Phase: Quick Starter Check (0–3m). Tone Alert: Fast, accessible animated educational summary of Cold War division and popular uprising.",
          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:10] Quick Check:</strong> Why did East German leader Walter Ulbricht and Nikita Khrushchev order the construction of the Berlin Wall in August 1961?</li><li><strong>[03:40] Hinge Question:</strong> How did Günter Schabowski's botched live press conference on the evening of 9 November 1989 accidentally trigger the immediate, euphoric fall of the Berlin Wall?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Quick Check:</strong> To halt the catastrophic \"brain drain\" of over 2.7 million skilled young East German workers, doctors, engineers, and intellectuals who were fleeing into democratic, prosperous West Berlin each year.</li><li><strong>Hinge Question:</strong> Schabowski was handed an unvetted bureaucratic note about relaxing travel visas. When an Italian journalist asked when the new rules took effect, an unprepared Schabowski fumbled his notes and replied: \"As far as I know, it takes effect immediately, without delay.\" Millions watched live on television; tens of thousands of East Berliners instantly converged on the checkpoints. Outnumbered border guards received no orders and stood down, opening the gates to history.</li></ol>"
