          "pathway": "Documentary",
          "teacher_guidance": "Comprehensive documentary detailing the December 1979 Soviet invasion of Afghanistan, the rise of the Mujahideen insurgency, the US boycott of the 1980 Moscow Olympics, President Carter's cancellation of grain sales, and the ten-year quagmire that bled the Soviet military of morale and treasure. Recommended Watch Window: 12:30–25:00 • Lesson Phase: Guided Instruction & The Afghan Trap. Tone Alert: Sobering military and diplomatic documentary featuring interviews with Zbigniew Brzezinski, Soviet veterans, and Mujahideen commanders.",
          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[06:30] Recall:</strong> In what month and year did the Soviet Red Army invade Afghanistan to prop up the embattled Marxist government in Kabul?</li><li><strong>[18:15] Explain:</strong> How did President Jimmy Carter respond diplomatically and economically to the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan, effectively bringing the era of Détente to an abrupt end?</li><li><strong>[31:40] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate why Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev later described the Soviet-Afghan War as a \"bleeding wound\": how did the conflict contribute to the internal collapse of the USSR?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> December 1979.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Carter condemned the invasion as the greatest threat to world peace since WWII. He withdrew the SALT II treaty from Senate ratification, placed an embargo on US grain and high-technology sales to the USSR, boycotted the 1980 Moscow Summer Olympic Games, and announced the \"Carter Doctrine\", stating the US would use military force to defend Persian Gulf oil fields.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Over 15,000 Soviet soldiers were killed and 50,000 wounded in a ten-year guerrilla war against an unseen mountain enemy. The war cost billions of rubles the stagnant Soviet economy could not afford, disproved the myth of Red Army invincibility, alienated the Muslim population of Soviet Central Asia, and produced thousands of traumatized, addicted veterans ('Afghantsy'). The failure discredited the Soviet military leadership and destroyed public confidence in the Communist regime.</li></ol>"
