          "model_answer": "<p>The summit meetings between Ronald Reagan and Mikhail Gorbachev between 1985 and 1988 were critically important for superpower relations because they dismantled decades of deep ideological hostility and established a genuine personal partnership. Beginning at Geneva in November 1985, the two leaders issued a landmark declaration that 'a nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought.' This personal rapport continued at Reykjavik in October 1986, where, despite disagreements over Reagan's Strategic Defense Initiative (SDI), both leaders debated abolishing all ballistic missiles, breaking the diplomatic stalemate that had frozen relations since 1979.</p><p>Furthermore, the summits were of historic importance because they produced the first genuine nuclear disarmament agreement in history, reversing the arms race. At the Washington Summit in December 1987, the leaders signed the Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces (INF) Treaty, eliminating 2,692 ground-launched missiles with rigorous on-site verification. When Reagan visited Moscow in May 1988, he publicly stated the USSR was no longer an 'evil empire.' Therefore, these summits were decisively important because they physically dismantled nuclear arsenals and peacefully de-escalated Cold War conflict.</p>"
